            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Public Works</strong><br>\nAs emperor, Titus initiated several public works projects to win the favor of the populace. One of his most notable contributions was completing the Flavian Amphitheatre, known as the Colosseum, initiated by his father. He also built public baths and restored areas damaged by the fire of 64 AD, demonstrating his commitment to improving Roman society.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Emperor's Inauguration</strong><br>\nUpon becoming emperor in 79 AD, Titus was well-received by the Roman people. His inauguration was celebrated with costly games that lasted for a hundred days. These games featured gladiatorial contests, animal fights, and naval battles, showcasing the splendor and might of Rome under his rule.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Handling Disasters</strong><br>\nTitus's short reign was marked by several natural disasters, including the eruption of Mount Vesuvius in 79 AD and a fire in Rome in 80 AD. Despite the challenges, he handled these events with a humanitarian approach, providing relief efforts and assisting in rebuilding efforts, which increased his standing among the Romans.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
