          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Relationship with the Senate</strong><br>\nTitus maintained a cooperative relationship with the Roman Senate, unlike many of his predecessors. He restored respect to the senatorial office by consulting with the Senate on matters of state and avoiding autocratic rule. This relationship reflected his larger strategy of ruling with a blend of traditional Roman values and imperial authority.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Cultural Achievements</strong><br>\nDuring Titus's reign, the Roman Empire continued to flourish culturally. He supported the arts, literature, and science, which helped to maintain the cultural richness of the empire. The period is marked by a continuation of the Pax Romana, a state of relative peace and stability that allowed culture to thrive.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Succession</strong><br>\nTitus died of a fever on September 13, 81 AD, after ruling for just over two years. His death was sudden and unexpected, leading to rumors of foul play. Nevertheless, his legacy was largely positive due to his effective leadership and generous policies. He was succeeded by his brother Domitian, who had a markedly different style of governance.</p>\n"
              },
